<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip I, Archbishop of Cologne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1130–<a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Philip of Heinsberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip of Heinsberg was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> from 1167 and chancellor of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelnhausen-charter">Gelnhausen Charter</a> of 1180 granted him the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Engern</a>, the basis of the archbishops' later territorial rule there. He built castles, including Pyrmont, to secure it.</p></div>
